DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These two system calls are the raw kernel  interface  for  getting  and
       setting  thread capabilities.  Not only are these system calls specific
       to Linux, but the kernel API is likely to change and use of these  sys‐
       tem  calls (in particular the format of the _c_a_p___u_s_e_r___*___t types) is sub‐
       ject to extension with each kernel revision, but old programs will keep
       working.

       The portable interfaces are  ccaapp__sseett__pprroocc(3)  and  ccaapp__ggeett__pprroocc(3);  if
       possible, you should use those interfaces in applications; see NOTES.

   CCuurrrreenntt ddeettaaiillss
       Now that you have been warned, some current kernel details.  The struc‐
       tures are defined as follows.

           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_1  0x19980330
           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_U32S_1     1

                   /* V2 added in Linux 2.6.25; deprecated */
           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_2  0x20071026
           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_U32S_2     2

                   /* V3 added in Linux 2.6.26 */
           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_3  0x20080522
           #define _LINUX_CAPABILITY_U32S_3     2

           typedef struct __user_cap_header_struct {
              __u32 version;
              int pid;
           } *cap_user_header_t;

           typedef struct __user_cap_data_struct {
              __u32 effective;
              __u32 permitted;
              __u32 inheritable;
           } *cap_user_data_t;

       The  _e_f_f_e_c_t_i_v_e,  _p_e_r_m_i_t_t_e_d, and _i_n_h_e_r_i_t_a_b_l_e fields are bit masks of the
       capabilities defined in ccaappaabbiilliittiieess(7).  Note that  the  CCAAPP__**  values
       are  bit  indexes  and need to be bit‐shifted before ORing into the bit
       fields.  To define the structures for passing to the system  call,  you
       have   to   use   the   _s_t_r_u_c_t   _____u_s_e_r___c_a_p___h_e_a_d_e_r___s_t_r_u_c_t   and  _s_t_r_u_c_t
       _____u_s_e_r___c_a_p___d_a_t_a___s_t_r_u_c_t names because the typedefs are only pointers.

       Kernels prior to Linux 2.6.25 prefer 32‐bit capabilities  with  version
       __LLIINNUUXX__CCAAPPAABBIILLIITTYY__VVEERRSSIIOONN__11.   Linux  2.6.25  added  64‐bit  capability
       sets, with version __LLIINNUUXX__CCAAPPAABBIILLIITTYY__VVEERRSSIIOONN__22.  There was, however, an
       API glitch, and Linux 2.6.26 added __LLIINNUUXX__CCAAPPAABBIILLIITTYY__VVEERRSSIIOONN__33  to  fix
       the problem.

       Note that 64‐bit capabilities use _d_a_t_a_p_[_0_] and _d_a_t_a_p_[_1_], whereas 32‐bit
       capabilities use only _d_a_t_a_p_[_0_].

       On  kernels  that support file capabilities (VFS capabilities support),
       these system calls behave slightly differently.  This support was added
       as an option in Linux 2.6.24, and became fixed (nonoptional)  in  Linux
       2.6.33.

       For  ccaappggeett()  calls,  one can probe the capabilities of any process by
       specifying its process ID with the _h_d_r_p_-_>_p_i_d field value.

       For details on the data, see ccaappaabbiilliittiieess(7).

   WWiitthh VVFFSS ccaappaabbiilliittiieess ssuuppppoorrtt
       VFS capabilities employ a file extended attribute (see xxaattttrr(7)) to al‐
       low capabilities to be attached to executables.  This  privilege  model
       obsoletes kernel support for one process asynchronously setting the ca‐
       pabilities  of another.  That is, on kernels that have VFS capabilities
       support, when calling ccaappsseett(), the only permitted values for _h_d_r_p_-_>_p_i_d
       are 0 or, equivalently, the value returned by ggeettttiidd(2).

   WWiitthhoouutt VVFFSS ccaappaabbiilliittiieess ssuuppppoorrtt
       On older kernels that do not provide VFS capabilities support  ccaappsseett()
       can,  if  the  caller has the CCAAPP__SSEETTPPCCAAPP capability, be used to change
       not only the caller’s own capabilities, but also  the  capabilities  of
       other  threads.   The  call  operates on the capabilities of the thread
       specified by the _p_i_d field of _h_d_r_p when that is nonzero, or on the  ca‐
       pabilities  of the calling thread if _p_i_d is 0.  If _p_i_d refers to a sin‐
       gle‐threaded process, then  _p_i_d  can  be  specified  as  a  traditional
       process ID; operating on a thread of a multithreaded process requires a
       thread  ID  of  the  type returned by ggeettttiidd(2).  For ccaappsseett(), _p_i_d can
       also be: -1, meaning perform the  change  on  all  threads  except  the
       caller  and  iinniitt(1); or a value less than -1, in which case the change
       is applied to all members of the process group whose ID is _-_p_i_d.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, zero is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and  _e_r_r_n_o  is
       set to indicate the error.

       The calls fail with the error EEIINNVVAALL, and set the _v_e_r_s_i_o_n field of _h_d_r_p
       to  the  kernel preferred value of __LLIINNUUXX__CCAAPPAABBIILLIITTYY__VVEERRSSIIOONN__??  when an
       unsupported _v_e_r_s_i_o_n value is specified.  In this  way,  one  can  probe
       what the current preferred capability revision is.

EERRRROORRSS
       EEFFAAUULLTT Bad  memory  address.  _h_d_r_p must not be NULL.  _d_a_t_a_p may be NULL
              only when the user is trying to determine the preferred capabil‐
              ity version format supported by the kernel.

       EEIINNVVAALL One of the arguments was invalid.

       EEPPEERRMM  An attempt was made to add a capability to the permitted set, or
              to set a capability in the effective set that is not in the per‐
              mitted set.

       EEPPEERRMM  An attempt was made to add a capability to the inheritable  set,
              and either:

              •  that capability was not in the caller’s bounding set; or

              •  the  capability was not in the caller’s permitted set and the
                 caller lacked the CCAAPP__SSEETTPPCCAAPP  capability  in  its  effective
                 set.

       EEPPEERRMM  The  caller attempted to use ccaappsseett() to modify the capabilities
              of a thread other than itself, but lacked sufficient  privilege.
              For  kernels  supporting VFS capabilities, this is never permit‐
              ted.  For kernels lacking VFS support, the CCAAPP__SSEETTPPCCAAPP  capabil‐
              ity  is  required.   (A bug in kernels before Linux 2.6.11 meant
              that this error could also occur if a thread without this  capa‐
              bility  tried  to  change its own capabilities by specifying the
              _p_i_d field as a nonzero value (i.e., the value returned  by  ggeett‐‐
              ppiidd(2)) instead of 0.)

       EESSRRCCHH  No such thread.

